===Verb===
{{en-verb|run<,,ran,run> out}}

# {{senseid|en|use up, consume all}} {{lb|en|intransitive|or transitive with 'of'|idiomatic}} To [[use up]] or [[consume]] all (of something).
#: {{hypo|en|sell out<id:sell all available stock, stock out>|stock out}}
#: {{cot|en|run low|run short}}
#: {{ux|en|Get some more beer out of the fridge before we '''run out'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|If this hot weather continues, we will '''run out''' of ice cream.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en
|date=June 19, 2012
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=England 1-0 Ukraine
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/18181971
|page=
|passage=England keeper Joe Hart had to save smartly from the dangerous Andriy Yarmolenko, who also raised the hopes of the Donetsk crowd as he evaded several challenges in the area before '''running out''' of space.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 December 2|author=Paul Bigland|title=My weirdest and wackiest Rover yet|journal=Rail|page=68|text=Time is '''running out''', so I renounce a spin on a Class 387 for a fast run to Paddington on another Class 800 - a shame as the weather was perfect for pictures. Even so, it's enjoyable - boy, can those trains shift under the wires.}}
# {{senseid|en|expire, be all used up or consumed}} {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[expire]]; to come to an [[end]]; to be completely used up or consumed.
#: {{ux|en|My driving licence '''runs out''' next week, so I had better renew it now.}}
#: {{ux|en|The option will '''run out''' next week and I can't get it extended.}}
#: {{ux|en|Oh no! The wine has '''run out'''!}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=April 11, 2011
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Liverpool 3 - 0 Man City
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9449111.stm
|page=
|passage=Mario Balotelli replaced Tevez but his contribution was so negligible that he suffered the indignity of being substituted himself as time '''ran out''', a development that encapsulated a wretched 90 minutes for City and boss Roberto Mancini.}}
# {{senseid|en|get a batsman out, in cricket}} {{lb|en|cricket}} To get a batsman out (dismissed from play) via a [[runout]].
#: {{ux|en|Jackson was '''run out''' for a duck in the first over.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Stephan Shemilt|title=England win Cricket World Cup: Ben Stokes stars in dramatic finale against New Zealand|work=BBC Sport|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/cricket/48983890|location=London|date=14 July 2019|text=With three runs needed from two balls, Adil Rashid was '''run out''' coming back for a second. When Wood suffered the same fate from the final ball, the match was tied.}}
# {{senseid|en|extend (fabric or clothing)}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[extend]] a piece of material, or clothing.
#: {{ux|en|If I '''run out''' these curtains, they will fit the windows in the drawing room.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[conclude]] in, to [[end up]].
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=September 29, 2011
|author=Jon Smith
|title=Tottenham 3 - 1 Shamrock Rovers
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15014632.stm
|page=
|passage=Tottenham survived a scare as they fought back from 1-0 down to '''run out''' comfortable winners against Shamrock Rovers in the Europa League.}}
# {{senseid|en|force to leave, v.t.}} To force (someone or something) out of a location or state of being.
#: {{ux|en|If the mob thinks you did it, they'll '''run''' you '''out''' of town.}}
#: {{ux|en|They'll '''run''' us '''out''' of business doing that!}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To grow poor over time by spending all of one's money.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|printing}} To fill a line with [[quadrat]]s or [[full point]]s.
# {{senseid|en|literal, leave hurriedly on foot}} {{&lit|en|run|out}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|Thesaurus:leave}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''ran out''' of the room in tears.}}

